  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nA drying trend will begin to erode some of the stronger moisture\nalong the UT and CO state line today. Satellite water vapor\ndepicts a plume of drier southwest flow invading this afternoon.\nCumulus fields have sprouted on the terrain of the West Slope in\nthe sunny conditions. This will produce a few showers and\nthunderstorms this afternoon, but coverage will be more\nabbreviated than previous days this week. Hi res models are\nfavoring the Uintas and the terrain of northwest CO for\nconvection this afternoon. A few showers will still spring up on\nthe southern Divide and the San Juans, but dry air will\ndefinitely pose some inertia for these showers. This will also\nlimit the impacts of heavy rain, as flash flooding is not\nexpected to materialize either. Showers decay quickly this \nevening as drier air intrudes and afternoon heating quickly \ndiminishes. Temperatures will trend near normal this afternoon\nand Sunday.\n\nThe new work week starts off warm and mostly dry with some near\nzero precipitation chances on the mountains. A weak frontal\nboundary settles into the northern Great Basin Tuesday and\nWednesday. This could bring winds up a bit for our northern\ncounties Tuesday. Relative humidity values tend to stay above\ncritical thresholds for fire weather and winds are not\nparticularly strong either. More widespread shower activity\nreturns Wednesday beneath this frontal activity. PWAT values\ntrend around 140 percent of normal north of the Colorado River\nand taper to near normal down south. These are ample values for\na few stronger showers if we can line up some forcing along the\nfront, which glances our northern counties Wednesday afternoon. \n\nHigh pressure starts amplifying over the West late in the week.\nThis will potentially send some more triple digit values into\nthe region, primarily in the lower valleys of the desert and the\nFour Corners. Models also hint at some weak monsoonal flow\ndrifting beneath the high late in the week. These details are\njust napkin planning at this point, but look like targets of\nforecast concern in a mostly benign weather week.",
